--TEST--
`Core\Response::bytes` sends its buffer verbatim, including octets no text-shaped body member could carry -- ADR 0088 § 4, ADR 0009
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0088 § 4's fourth row is the member for a body that is not text, so the
// edge worth pinning is the one `text` cannot reach: a buffer holding a NUL
// and bytes that are not valid UTF-8 goes out unchanged and unmeasured. The
// write is read back through a capture -- § 5's redirection of the same output
// -- and re-hexed, because the bytes themselves are not something an exact
// expectation can spell.
bytes $png = Core\Encoding::fromHex("89504e470d0a1a0a00ff");
var $sent = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Response::bytes($png, "image/png");
});
echo Core\Encoding::toHex($sent as string as bytes), "\n";

// An empty body is a body: nothing is written, and nothing refuses. The
// content type still had to be spellable, which is the other parameter's
// business rather than this one's.
Core\Response::bytes("" as bytes, "application/octet-stream");
echo "[empty]\n";

// A media type with parameters is one string, not a shape: § 4 says the type
// is "as given", so whatever the program spelled is what is declared.
Core\Response::bytes("id,name" as bytes, "text/csv; charset=utf-8; header=present");
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
89504e470d0a1a0a00ff
[empty]
id,name
